## Two research findings that changed the work
**The conversion claim doesn't hold.** The review said she converted to the LDS church at 18. Rolling Stone reports those rumors were never confirmed. The verified record is better evidence anyway: she affirmed LDS in comments early, declined every marker in March 2024 ("I don't wear garments. I didn't get married in the temple"), and by July 2025 said flatly she is not Mormon. She never argued theology, she dismantled the category — the dishes line pointed at a church.

**I rejected the review's statistic.** It proposed "7% of stay-at-home mothers are Black vs. 49% white" as proof Black women were excluded from homemaking. Pew's actual rate data shows 27% of Black mothers vs 26% of white mothers — near parity. The 7/49 figure is population composition, not a rate. I made the historical point qualitatively instead, anchored to Kay Wicker in TheGrio (3 Aug 2026), whose "any one box" line lands directly on Nara's own "narrow boxes" quote.

